WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.295 --> 00:00:06.920
CHRIS CRALL: Welcome
to today's session

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.940
on integrating VM workloads
into Anthos Service Mesh.

00:00:10.940 --> 00:00:11.810
I'm Chris Crall.

00:00:11.810 --> 00:00:14.810
I'm a Product Manager
in Google Cloud.

00:00:14.810 --> 00:00:16.590
I've been with Google
for about six years,

00:00:16.590 --> 00:00:19.350
and I've been focusing on
services most of that time.

00:00:19.350 --> 00:00:21.380
And for the last two
years, specifically

00:00:21.380 --> 00:00:23.875
on Anthos Service Mesh.

00:00:23.875 --> 00:00:25.250
SVEN MAWSON: And
I'm Sven Mawson.

00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.875
I'm a Software Engineer
in Google Cloud.

00:00:27.875 --> 00:00:29.750
I'm one of the founders
of the Istio project.

00:00:29.750 --> 00:00:33.050
And I've been focused really on
expanding the reach of meshes

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.160
to multiple clusters and,
in this case, to VMs.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:39.950
CHRIS CRALL: So before we get
into the details of how we're

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:42.403
integrating VM workloads
into Service Mesh,

00:00:42.403 --> 00:00:43.820
I want to give a
little background

00:00:43.820 --> 00:00:46.670
and a little motivation
on why we're doing it.

00:00:46.670 --> 00:00:51.780
So one of the factors behind
this is the microservice trend.

00:00:51.780 --> 00:00:53.360
This is a big topic
in the industry.

00:00:53.360 --> 00:00:55.680
It has been for some time.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.150
And people talk about a lot
of things about microservices,

00:00:59.150 --> 00:01:01.790
how when you break the
monolithic application up

00:01:01.790 --> 00:01:06.130
into smaller services,
your teams can move faster.

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:08.320
They can even decide
which language

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.640
each team wants to write in.

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.320
Some might write in Java.

00:01:11.320 --> 00:01:12.910
Some might write in Python.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:14.920
It doesn't matter because
they're broken apart

00:01:14.920 --> 00:01:18.760
and operate
independently over APIs.

00:01:18.760 --> 00:01:21.800
They can roll out new
versions on their own.

00:01:21.800 --> 00:01:25.450
They don't need to roll out the
whole application at one time.

00:01:25.450 --> 00:01:27.080
It might be more scalable.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:31.510
They can swap out something
that has been purpose-built

00:01:31.510 --> 00:01:33.530
for maybe a SaaS service.

00:01:33.530 --> 00:01:36.850
So there are tons of benefits
behind microservices.

00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:39.670
But the bottom line is
what you're trying to do

00:01:39.670 --> 00:01:43.330
is roll out new solutions
for your customers.

00:01:43.330 --> 00:01:46.300
You're trying to get those
solutions out there faster

00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.070
and make them
better, either higher

00:01:48.070 --> 00:01:50.350
reliability, more functionality.

00:01:50.350 --> 00:01:52.990
Whatever it is, you're
trying to roll out solutions

00:01:52.990 --> 00:01:55.450
to your customers.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:59.170
However, this new architecture
comes with some drawbacks

00:01:59.170 --> 00:02:01.480
as well, right?

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.233
As you break things apart,
and now your services

00:02:04.233 --> 00:02:05.650
are doing a lot
more communication

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:06.620
than they used to.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.400
So you have to know how
they're communicating.

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:12.730
You may need to secure
that communication.

00:02:12.730 --> 00:02:14.720
Since the services
are independent,

00:02:14.720 --> 00:02:17.350
then you may have them
logging in different places.

00:02:17.350 --> 00:02:19.340
So it's harder to debug.

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:23.830
So all these things can lead to
some confusion, some downtime,

00:02:23.830 --> 00:02:26.720
and ultimately,
unhappy customers.

00:02:26.720 --> 00:02:29.540
So what can we do to solve this?

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:33.860
Well, one thing we can do
is introduce a service mesh.

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:39.200
Now a service mesh, I've
defined here in generic terms.

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:40.830
And it provides
several attributes.

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:44.510
One is, first off, it
should be transparent.

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:48.470
It should allow us to roll out
a mesh and not have to go in

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.480
and change application code.

00:02:50.480 --> 00:02:54.680
So you don't have to link in
libraries and things like that.

00:02:54.680 --> 00:02:56.270
As we talked about
with microservices,

00:02:56.270 --> 00:02:57.710
it should be
language-independent.

00:02:57.710 --> 00:02:59.850
So it shouldn't matter
whether your component

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:02.030
is written in Go or Java.

00:03:02.030 --> 00:03:04.820
It should work in
the service mesh.

00:03:04.820 --> 00:03:06.320
And then you want
that service mesh

00:03:06.320 --> 00:03:09.080
to be able to automate a
bunch of things for you.

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:12.380
Whether it's network traffic
or logging or whatever it is,

00:03:12.380 --> 00:03:14.460
it should be automatic.

00:03:14.460 --> 00:03:18.160
So those are the kinds of things
a service mesh can give you.

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:21.240
So let's talk about
the Anthos Service Mesh

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:23.790
and what it does
provide for you.

00:03:23.790 --> 00:03:25.650
Now, Anthos Service
Mesh-- and sometimes,

00:03:25.650 --> 00:03:28.050
we use the acronym ASM--

00:03:28.050 --> 00:03:30.060
is built on top of Istio.

00:03:30.060 --> 00:03:32.340
It's powered by Istio,
which is an open source

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:34.290
project that Google started.

00:03:34.290 --> 00:03:37.920
And it works with
multiple partners.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:40.110
We talk about three
main attributes

00:03:40.110 --> 00:03:42.180
of Anthos Service Mesh.

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.550
The first thing is
uniform observability.

00:03:44.550 --> 00:03:47.130
So the idea is that
we can collect metrics

00:03:47.130 --> 00:03:50.010
on all communication
going on in the mesh.

00:03:50.010 --> 00:03:51.510
We know which
services are talking

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:53.440
to which other services.

00:03:53.440 --> 00:03:55.020
We know what the
requests per second

00:03:55.020 --> 00:03:56.670
are between those services.

00:03:56.670 --> 00:03:58.860
We can find the error rates.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:02.460
And that can be done just
instantaneously as well as

00:04:02.460 --> 00:04:04.410
over a time series.

00:04:04.410 --> 00:04:07.870
We can even set SLOs, both
on availability and latency.

00:04:07.870 --> 00:04:09.480
So we have lots of
information about

00:04:09.480 --> 00:04:13.850
what is going on in the mesh
from a traffic perspective.

00:04:13.850 --> 00:04:16.120
Second, we have
operational agility.

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.260
So we are in the network.

00:04:18.260 --> 00:04:20.180
And we can control
that network traffic.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:22.089
So we can do lots of things.

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:25.780
We can do canary deployments,
or blue-green deployments.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:27.050
We can do load balancing.

00:04:27.050 --> 00:04:30.490
And we can do load balancing
with a variety of algorithms.

00:04:30.490 --> 00:04:32.050
For testing
purposes, we can even

00:04:32.050 --> 00:04:34.720
do fault injections so you can
make sure your services are

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.250
resilient.

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:39.360
And we can even set quotas.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:41.700
The third attribute is security.

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:45.360
So first, we have identities
for the different services.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:48.420
And with those identities,
we can get certificates.

00:04:48.420 --> 00:04:51.870
Once we have those certificates,
then we can enable mTLS.

00:04:51.870 --> 00:04:55.020
So with mTLS, now we
know which services

00:04:55.020 --> 00:04:58.890
are talking to which services in
a secure cryptographic manner.

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:00.630
And all of the data
can be encrypted

00:05:00.630 --> 00:05:04.600
across the connection, so nobody
else can see what's going on.

00:05:04.600 --> 00:05:06.280
With mTLS, then,
on top of that, we

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:08.350
can set access control policies.

00:05:08.350 --> 00:05:11.770
We have simple things
like allow and deny lists,

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:15.560
but we have lots of other
access control policies as well.

00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:18.590
So these are the three big
areas of Anthos Service Mesh.

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:25.010
So let's talk about how
that plays with VMs now.

00:05:25.010 --> 00:05:26.660
So we've known from
the very beginning

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:31.160
that VMs were critical in the
service mesh because 80% to 90%

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:35.000
of the workloads in a
customer environment

00:05:35.000 --> 00:05:39.350
are running on VMs or
bare metal machines.

00:05:39.350 --> 00:05:42.813
Containers in Kubernetes provide
a lot of wonderful benefits.

00:05:42.813 --> 00:05:45.230
But again, most of the workloads
are still running on VMs,

00:05:45.230 --> 00:05:48.616
so it's critical we integrate
those into the environment.

00:05:48.616 --> 00:05:52.150
In this diagram, I'm showing
a high-level architecture

00:05:52.150 --> 00:05:54.080
of what the mesh looks like.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:57.260
So on the left is a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:05:57.260 --> 00:05:59.890
And there are some control
plane components running there,

00:05:59.890 --> 00:06:01.960
mainly the Istio
sidecar injector,

00:06:01.960 --> 00:06:06.430
which puts proxies beside the
pods for the services running

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:08.190
in the cluster.

00:06:08.190 --> 00:06:11.070
Across the bottom, I'm also
representing managed control

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.910
plane components.

00:06:12.910 --> 00:06:15.990
These are Stackdriver
for logging and metrics,

00:06:15.990 --> 00:06:18.780
Traffic Director for
controlling the traffic,

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.710
and the mesh CA
for certificates.

00:06:21.710 --> 00:06:23.230
But in the middle are the VMs.

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:25.250
And again, this is what
this session is about,

00:06:25.250 --> 00:06:29.000
is how we incorporate
these VMs into this mesh.

00:06:29.000 --> 00:06:30.980
Now, there are a
variety of reasons

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:33.080
that workloads are on VMs.

00:06:33.080 --> 00:06:37.430
In some cases, that's just
where they need to run.

00:06:37.430 --> 00:06:41.330
Databases have been tuned
and run very well on VMs.

00:06:41.330 --> 00:06:43.227
Moving those to
containers may or may not

00:06:43.227 --> 00:06:44.810
give you the same
kind of performance.

00:06:44.810 --> 00:06:46.490
So a lot of times,
I see customers

00:06:46.490 --> 00:06:50.280
that are still running
their data stores in VMs.

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:52.820
And it makes a lot of sense.

00:06:52.820 --> 00:06:55.080
Second, we have
packaged applications.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:06:57.800
So some vendors don't
support their applications

00:06:57.800 --> 00:06:59.550
running in containers.

00:06:59.550 --> 00:07:01.460
So this particular
company may have

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:04.520
a ERP or a CRM application.

00:07:04.520 --> 00:07:06.118
And they have to run in on a VM.

00:07:06.118 --> 00:07:07.910
In fact, usually, the
vendor will tell them

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:10.220
exactly what distro and
what version of the VM

00:07:10.220 --> 00:07:12.417
they have to run.

00:07:12.417 --> 00:07:14.000
Most importantly,
though, are probably

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:15.470
the legacy applications.

00:07:15.470 --> 00:07:17.360
These are the
applications that you've

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:19.800
built to run your business.

00:07:19.800 --> 00:07:21.750
And those are still
running on VMs.

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:24.300
They probably will
be for a long time.

00:07:24.300 --> 00:07:28.220
There's no way that a company
has the budget, the time,

00:07:28.220 --> 00:07:32.120
and the engineers to move
everything over to containers,

00:07:32.120 --> 00:07:33.540
even if that made sense.

00:07:33.540 --> 00:07:35.498
So VMs are going to be
around for a long time.

00:07:35.498 --> 00:07:37.040
They're the majority
of the workload.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:41.290
And we have to incorporate
those into the mesh.

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:45.250
So let's start diving into
how we're going to do that.

00:07:45.250 --> 00:07:47.850
And we're going to talk about
managed instance groups.

00:07:47.850 --> 00:07:51.770
Now, managed instance
groups are a feature of GCE.

00:07:51.770 --> 00:07:55.860
And they are a set of VMs
that all look the same

00:07:55.860 --> 00:07:58.077
and run the same
kind of workload.

00:07:58.077 --> 00:07:59.910
So for example, if you
have a web front end,

00:07:59.910 --> 00:08:03.080
you might want 10 VMs
running that web front end.

00:08:03.080 --> 00:08:05.090
You might even
want to autoscale.

00:08:05.090 --> 00:08:08.910
You're doing load
balancing, things like that.

00:08:08.910 --> 00:08:13.400
So the way that managed instance
groups are created in GCE

00:08:13.400 --> 00:08:16.630
is you first create
a instance template.

00:08:16.630 --> 00:08:19.640
Now, this template defines
what the VM looks like.

00:08:19.640 --> 00:08:21.050
How much memory do I want?

00:08:21.050 --> 00:08:23.490
How cores chords do I want?

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:25.730
What kind of storage, what
kind of persistent disks

00:08:25.730 --> 00:08:28.820
I want, what network the
VMs are going to run on--

00:08:28.820 --> 00:08:32.488
all of those things go
in the instance template.

00:08:32.488 --> 00:08:34.530
Once they're in there,
the managed instance group

00:08:34.530 --> 00:08:35.610
is created.

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:38.789
And the user tells
the management group

00:08:38.789 --> 00:08:41.740
how many VMs should be created.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:44.670
I want 20 VMs, or I
want a hundred VMs.

00:08:44.670 --> 00:08:46.170
Or you can even do autoscaling--

00:08:46.170 --> 00:08:51.000
I want somewhere
between 15 and 35 VMs.

00:08:51.000 --> 00:08:53.700
So the managed instance
group will spin up the VMs

00:08:53.700 --> 00:08:56.850
and control how they're created.

00:08:56.850 --> 00:08:59.160
The wonderful
thing about MIGs is

00:08:59.160 --> 00:09:01.920
because that managed
instance group is controlling

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.770
the lifecycle, it can do
health checks on the VMs

00:09:04.770 --> 00:09:07.080
and kill VMs that
aren't working correctly

00:09:07.080 --> 00:09:08.450
and then restart them.

00:09:08.450 --> 00:09:11.850
Again, autoscaling events-- if
you have autoscaling turned on,

00:09:11.850 --> 00:09:15.400
it will start and stop the
VMs depending on the load.

00:09:15.400 --> 00:09:16.318
So what we're doing--

00:09:16.318 --> 00:09:18.360
and Sven will go into a
lot more detail on this--

00:09:18.360 --> 00:09:22.590
is we're adding some features
to the instance template

00:09:22.590 --> 00:09:26.550
so that when the managed
instance group creates VMs,

00:09:26.550 --> 00:09:28.410
it adds the mesh
software to them

00:09:28.410 --> 00:09:31.020
and allows them
to join the mesh.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:33.403
So Sven is going to go
into more detail now.

00:09:33.403 --> 00:09:35.070
SVEN MAWSON: So I'm
going to try to give

00:09:35.070 --> 00:09:37.650
a brief overview of the
architecture of the system.

00:09:37.650 --> 00:09:40.350
And I'm going to use an example
that we're actually going

00:09:40.350 --> 00:09:42.220
to be using in a demo later.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:45.870
So here, we have a GKE cluster
that's running Anthos Service

00:09:45.870 --> 00:09:47.470
Mesh 1.6.

00:09:47.470 --> 00:09:50.230
And it has the online
boutique sample application

00:09:50.230 --> 00:09:51.300
installed in it.

00:09:51.300 --> 00:09:53.680
But we actually took out
the product catalog service,

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:55.590
and we moved that
over into a MIG

00:09:55.590 --> 00:09:59.090
instead of being run
in a Kubernetes pod.

00:09:59.090 --> 00:10:02.240
And I want to talk a little
bit about how Anthos Service

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.550
Mesh works and the
services it relies on,

00:10:04.550 --> 00:10:07.580
because I think it's important
to understand just how

00:10:07.580 --> 00:10:10.430
many different services are
involved in making the mesh

00:10:10.430 --> 00:10:12.600
actually work the
way you want to.

00:10:12.600 --> 00:10:14.900
So the first thing that
happens when the MIG starts up

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:16.580
is that it starts
up this agent that's

00:10:16.580 --> 00:10:19.760
running in the MIG in the VM.

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:22.820
And that agent connects
over to the DNS server

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:25.370
running in Kubernetes,
KubeDNS, and acts

00:10:25.370 --> 00:10:27.450
as a proxy for that DNS server.

00:10:27.450 --> 00:10:30.140
And this is used to give
service discovery to the VM

00:10:30.140 --> 00:10:32.160
just like you get service
discovery in pods.

00:10:32.160 --> 00:10:33.920
So the same type
of mesh services

00:10:33.920 --> 00:10:38.180
will be resolved to IP
addresses in that mesh.

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:39.710
The second service
it talks to is

00:10:39.710 --> 00:10:45.410
the mesh CA, which is used to
mint certificates for the VM.

00:10:45.410 --> 00:10:47.540
And the way that this
works in practice

00:10:47.540 --> 00:10:51.350
is that the agent is actually
acting kind of as a proxy.

00:10:51.350 --> 00:10:54.190
So it runs a secret
discovery service

00:10:54.190 --> 00:10:56.060
that that proxy can connect to.

00:10:56.060 --> 00:10:59.300
And it proxies that
over to the mesh CA.

00:10:59.300 --> 00:11:03.270
And it gets the CA
certificates from there.

00:11:03.270 --> 00:11:05.390
So the agent then starts
up the Envoy proxy

00:11:05.390 --> 00:11:07.490
that's running inside the VM.

00:11:07.490 --> 00:11:10.430
And the proxy is configured
to talk to the control plane

00:11:10.430 --> 00:11:11.320
component.

00:11:11.320 --> 00:11:15.480
And in this picture, it's
Istiod running in the cluster.

00:11:15.480 --> 00:11:18.600
And it's also configured to send
telemetry over to Stackdriver.

00:11:18.600 --> 00:11:22.310
So this sends all of the logs,
metrics, traces, and actually

00:11:22.310 --> 00:11:24.350
also edges, which we use
for our topology graph

00:11:24.350 --> 00:11:26.385
over to Stackdriver.

00:11:26.385 --> 00:11:28.130
And it, of course,
connects to the agent

00:11:28.130 --> 00:11:32.480
for those certificates so it
can get mutual TLS bootstrapped.

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:36.258
The application itself is then
proxied via that Envoy proxy.

00:11:36.258 --> 00:11:37.800
And that can be done
one of two ways.

00:11:37.800 --> 00:11:40.790
It's either using IP tables,
which is a transparent proxy

00:11:40.790 --> 00:11:42.290
mechanism, or it
can actually also

00:11:42.290 --> 00:11:44.570
be run at white box mode,
where the application knows

00:11:44.570 --> 00:11:48.681
about the proxy and sort of
manually interacts with it.

00:11:48.681 --> 00:11:51.470
ASM is also relying on
the Kubernetes API server

00:11:51.470 --> 00:11:52.730
for configuration storage.

00:11:52.730 --> 00:11:54.350
That's actually
not pictured here.

00:11:54.350 --> 00:11:56.773
But that's also another key
component of the system.

00:11:56.773 --> 00:11:58.940
You need a place to store
your configuration, things

00:11:58.940 --> 00:12:03.200
like service definitions,
authorization policies,

00:12:03.200 --> 00:12:04.850
the routing information.

00:12:04.850 --> 00:12:07.010
Basically, all the
configuration for the features

00:12:07.010 --> 00:12:09.478
that Anthos Service
Mesh offers is provided

00:12:09.478 --> 00:12:10.770
in that configurations drawing.

00:12:14.340 --> 00:12:16.380
So let's walk through
how we actually

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:17.525
add this MIG to the mesh.

00:12:17.525 --> 00:12:19.900
And we're going to look at
the four different steps here.

00:12:19.900 --> 00:12:23.220
So step 1 is configuring
the VM's identity.

00:12:23.220 --> 00:12:25.740
Step 2 is starting up
the MIG and connecting it

00:12:25.740 --> 00:12:27.750
to the services it needs.

00:12:27.750 --> 00:12:30.960
And then steps 3 and 4
are registering that VM

00:12:30.960 --> 00:12:35.340
as an endpoint in the mesh and
selecting that endpoint, or all

00:12:35.340 --> 00:12:38.765
the endpoints that are relevant,
into relevant services.

00:12:38.765 --> 00:12:40.348
So let's walk through
them one by one.

00:12:42.532 --> 00:12:44.240
So the first thing
we're going to look at

00:12:44.240 --> 00:12:45.950
is the identity space.

00:12:45.950 --> 00:12:47.780
And this gets a
little bit complicated

00:12:47.780 --> 00:12:50.600
because, on GCP,
the VMs are actually

00:12:50.600 --> 00:12:53.940
running as GCP service
accounts, not as

00:12:53.940 --> 00:12:55.247
Kubernetes service accounts.

00:12:55.247 --> 00:12:56.580
And these have a different form.

00:12:56.580 --> 00:12:58.110
So a GCP service account--

00:12:58.110 --> 00:13:01.160
there's really two different
main ways that you'd run a VM.

00:13:01.160 --> 00:13:03.380
You can either use the
default service account

00:13:03.380 --> 00:13:06.380
for the VM, which is just
project-number-compute.

00:13:06.380 --> 00:13:09.300
Or you can use a named service
account that you created,

00:13:09.300 --> 00:13:12.080
which is a
service-account-name@project-id.

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:15.290
And these would be the
identity that the VM starts up

00:13:15.290 --> 00:13:17.660
with and is available through
the metadata server built

00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:19.500
into GCE.

00:13:19.500 --> 00:13:22.310
But in Anthos Service Mesh, we
want to use mesh identities.

00:13:22.310 --> 00:13:26.720
So mesh identities are
basically three parts.

00:13:26.720 --> 00:13:29.540
They are a trust domain,
or a workload pool,

00:13:29.540 --> 00:13:32.113
which identifies the
source of the identities.

00:13:32.113 --> 00:13:34.280
And then there are a namespace
and a service account

00:13:34.280 --> 00:13:35.700
within that namespace.

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:37.760
And so we need to map
between these two.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:41.240
And ASM for GCE provides
a default mapping

00:13:41.240 --> 00:13:45.020
that will map those
service accounts from GCP

00:13:45.020 --> 00:13:48.950
over into service
accounts in Anthos.

00:13:48.950 --> 00:13:51.740
We are also working on making
this configurable so you

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:53.593
can map into the
namespaces that you want to

00:13:53.593 --> 00:13:55.135
rather than using
the default mapping

00:13:55.135 --> 00:13:59.050
if that doesn't work for you.

00:13:59.050 --> 00:14:04.020
OK, so now we need to actually
get that instance group started

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:07.150
and verify that everything
is actually working.

00:14:07.150 --> 00:14:10.170
So we do this using the
mechanisms that Chris was just

00:14:10.170 --> 00:14:11.337
talking about earlier.

00:14:11.337 --> 00:14:13.920
We use an instance template that
we configure with the service

00:14:13.920 --> 00:14:15.540
proxy.

00:14:15.540 --> 00:14:16.780
We give it the configuration.

00:14:16.780 --> 00:14:19.830
It needs to know what
entity it needs to use.

00:14:19.830 --> 00:14:22.830
And then we connect it to
the control plane components

00:14:22.830 --> 00:14:24.255
that I was just talking about.

00:14:24.255 --> 00:14:26.630
Once it's running, we can
verify that it works correctly.

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:28.620
We can check that it's
connected to the control plane,

00:14:28.620 --> 00:14:30.140
and it's getting configuration.

00:14:30.140 --> 00:14:31.790
We can verify it
has a certificate,

00:14:31.790 --> 00:14:33.318
and rotation is set up properly.

00:14:33.318 --> 00:14:35.360
And we can make sure that
telemetry is going over

00:14:35.360 --> 00:14:37.943
to Stackdriver and even go and
actually look at that telemetry

00:14:37.943 --> 00:14:39.920
if we want to in
Stackdriver to make sure it

00:14:39.920 --> 00:14:41.480
looks like what we expect.

00:14:45.400 --> 00:14:48.360
So we have this running, But
we aren't able to actually call

00:14:48.360 --> 00:14:49.290
the VM yet.

00:14:49.290 --> 00:14:50.990
So we can't route
traffic to it yet.

00:14:50.990 --> 00:14:54.060
So to get traffic to the
VM, we need to register.

00:14:54.060 --> 00:14:56.298
And in Anthos Service
Mesh, we do this

00:14:56.298 --> 00:14:57.840
through a decoupling
mechanism that's

00:14:57.840 --> 00:15:01.560
very similar to how pods and
services work in Kubernetes.

00:15:01.560 --> 00:15:04.080
The equivalent in Anthos
Service Mesh for VMs

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:06.310
is WorkloadEntry
and ServiceEntry.

00:15:06.310 --> 00:15:09.270
And a WorkloadEntry is
basically a named endpoint

00:15:09.270 --> 00:15:10.560
in a namespace.

00:15:10.560 --> 00:15:14.520
It gives us a way to attach
labels and attach a service

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:16.050
account to an endpoint.

00:15:16.050 --> 00:15:17.670
And then that thing
can be selected

00:15:17.670 --> 00:15:19.503
by the next thing we're
going to talk about,

00:15:19.503 --> 00:15:22.810
which is a ServiceEntry.

00:15:22.810 --> 00:15:27.910
So a ServiceEntry basically
lets us create named mappings

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:29.470
to endpoints in the
same way that you

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:31.720
do with Kubernetes services,
except that it can also

00:15:31.720 --> 00:15:33.270
select workload entries.

00:15:33.270 --> 00:15:34.720
So just like a
Kubernetes service,

00:15:34.720 --> 00:15:38.920
it has a port, or a
set of ports, actually.

00:15:38.920 --> 00:15:40.600
It has a workload
selector that says

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:42.550
what endpoints are selected.

00:15:42.550 --> 00:15:45.910
And in ASM, we also let
you specify the host

00:15:45.910 --> 00:15:48.500
as a generic opaque string.

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:50.500
It doesn't have to just
be a Kubernetes service.

00:15:50.500 --> 00:15:52.210
We can actually use
other types too.

00:15:52.210 --> 00:15:55.210
But for using the same
service as a Kubernetes one,

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:58.610
you can just specify the
host, like we did here.

00:15:58.610 --> 00:16:01.450
So that's how this all
works and the architecture.

00:16:01.450 --> 00:16:02.860
Let's look at a demo next.

00:16:02.860 --> 00:16:04.360
So in the demo,
we're actually going

00:16:04.360 --> 00:16:08.050
to set up exactly the
architecture I just

00:16:08.050 --> 00:16:09.160
talked about.

00:16:09.160 --> 00:16:12.190
And we're going to show off the
three pillars of Anthos Service

00:16:12.190 --> 00:16:17.290
Mesh, the observability
features, the agility,

00:16:17.290 --> 00:16:19.540
networking, and traffic
management features,

00:16:19.540 --> 00:16:20.920
and the security features.

00:16:20.920 --> 00:16:24.070
So we'll look at each of
those in turn in this demo.

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:27.256
So let's see our demo of VMs
running with Anthos Service

00:16:27.256 --> 00:16:28.050
Mesh.

00:16:28.050 --> 00:16:32.630
So in this demo, we're going
to show the three main pillars

00:16:32.630 --> 00:16:35.930
of Anthos Service Mesh, the
observability features here

00:16:35.930 --> 00:16:38.875
in the form of the dashboards,
the traffic management

00:16:38.875 --> 00:16:40.500
capabilities, which
we're going to show

00:16:40.500 --> 00:16:43.420
a little bit of canarying
of a new version,

00:16:43.420 --> 00:16:44.820
and the security features.

00:16:44.820 --> 00:16:47.040
And here, we're going to
show authorization policies.

00:16:47.040 --> 00:16:48.665
And this just gives
you a little flavor

00:16:48.665 --> 00:16:51.750
of what you can do with
Anthos Service Mesh for VMs.

00:16:51.750 --> 00:16:54.870
It's not meant to be a complete
demo of all the good things you

00:16:54.870 --> 00:16:56.430
get from it.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:58.910
So let's get into it.

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:00.410
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:17:00.410 --> 00:17:03.000
is look at what we
have created so far.

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:05.720
So we have a cluster
here, a GKE cluster,

00:17:05.720 --> 00:17:08.210
running Anthos Service Mesh.

00:17:08.210 --> 00:17:11.359
This cluster also has the online
boutique sample application

00:17:11.359 --> 00:17:15.700
installed, except we don't have
the product catalog service.

00:17:15.700 --> 00:17:18.710
So we're also running a
managed instance group

00:17:18.710 --> 00:17:20.119
with two VMs in it.

00:17:20.119 --> 00:17:23.150
And they are running
the product catalog

00:17:23.150 --> 00:17:25.190
service for the online
boutique instead

00:17:25.190 --> 00:17:26.369
of running it in clusters.

00:17:26.369 --> 00:17:29.040
So this can show you how
you can run VMs combined

00:17:29.040 --> 00:17:32.650
with your Kubernetes services.

00:17:32.650 --> 00:17:34.870
But right now, we don't
have these things hooked up

00:17:34.870 --> 00:17:35.720
together.

00:17:35.720 --> 00:17:38.710
So right now, this is failing
because the mesh doesn't

00:17:38.710 --> 00:17:39.710
know about this VM.

00:17:39.710 --> 00:17:41.210
So the first thing
we're going to do

00:17:41.210 --> 00:17:43.210
is actually show that
registration step.

00:17:43.210 --> 00:17:46.058
So we're going create
a WorkloadEntry

00:17:46.058 --> 00:17:47.350
and a ServiceEntry for the VMs.

00:17:47.350 --> 00:17:48.430
So let's look at these.

00:17:53.710 --> 00:17:55.990
So this is a WorkloadEntry
for the product catalog

00:17:55.990 --> 00:17:59.477
service, V1 of it,
running on an IP address.

00:17:59.477 --> 00:18:00.810
So let's go ahead and create it.

00:18:07.020 --> 00:18:13.227
And let's also look at
the ServiceEntry for this.

00:18:13.227 --> 00:18:15.560
And this ServiceEntry basically
says the product catalog

00:18:15.560 --> 00:18:21.200
service is all of the workload
entries with the label selected

00:18:21.200 --> 00:18:23.960
below, where the app is
product catalog service.

00:18:23.960 --> 00:18:26.210
So this will select everything,
and it's not actually

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:27.043
used in the version.

00:18:27.043 --> 00:18:29.190
We'll see what version
is used for later.

00:18:29.190 --> 00:18:31.670
And this is basically the
same as a Kubernetes service

00:18:31.670 --> 00:18:34.630
except it can select workload
entries as well as Kubernetes

00:18:34.630 --> 00:18:36.133
pods.

00:18:36.133 --> 00:18:37.550
So let's go ahead
and create that.

00:18:44.508 --> 00:18:47.290
And now that we've created
that, our online boutique

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:50.443
should be working again.

00:18:50.443 --> 00:18:51.110
And there it is.

00:18:51.110 --> 00:18:52.282
It's working.

00:18:52.282 --> 00:18:54.740
So once we have this up and
running, the first thing we get

00:18:54.740 --> 00:18:56.250
is all the telemetry features.

00:18:56.250 --> 00:18:59.030
So the telemetry--
because there's

00:18:59.030 --> 00:19:03.400
load going through this
service, all that telemetry

00:19:03.400 --> 00:19:04.400
is going to Stackdriver.

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:06.310
And we can actually see it
in the Anthos Service Mesh

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:06.830
dashboard.

00:19:06.830 --> 00:19:09.163
So here's the dashboard for
the product catalog service.

00:19:09.163 --> 00:19:12.230
We can see the requests per
second, error rates, latencies,

00:19:12.230 --> 00:19:14.915
CPU usage, things like that.

00:19:14.915 --> 00:19:16.790
You can also use all
the other great features

00:19:16.790 --> 00:19:18.230
of Anthos Service
Mesh dashboards,

00:19:18.230 --> 00:19:23.080
the Health ability to create a
SLOs, look at detailed metrics,

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:25.910
look at connected services,
do more diagnostics,

00:19:25.910 --> 00:19:26.880
that kind of stuff.

00:19:26.880 --> 00:19:28.840
So all the same
features you expect,

00:19:28.840 --> 00:19:33.220
but now available for a
service running in a VM.

00:19:33.220 --> 00:19:35.930
So the next step is, let's
do some traffic management.

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:38.710
So we want to roll
out V2 of our service.

00:19:38.710 --> 00:19:41.085
And the only
difference in V1 and V2

00:19:41.085 --> 00:19:43.210
is we're going to have a
slightly different product

00:19:43.210 --> 00:19:45.808
catalog in this case.

00:19:45.808 --> 00:19:47.350
We're going to have
different product

00:19:47.350 --> 00:19:50.860
description and a different
price for this typewriter.

00:19:50.860 --> 00:19:52.150
So let's go ahead and do that.

00:19:52.150 --> 00:19:53.650
So the way we do
this is we're going

00:19:53.650 --> 00:19:56.193
to create another WorkloadEntry.

00:19:59.900 --> 00:20:03.220
And here, we use V2 as
the version instead of V1.

00:20:03.220 --> 00:20:04.670
And otherwise, it's the same.

00:20:04.670 --> 00:20:07.515
And we don't have to actually
change the ServiceEntry.

00:20:07.515 --> 00:20:09.640
We just add a new WorkloadEntry,
which is basically

00:20:09.640 --> 00:20:10.604
adding a new endpoint.

00:20:19.895 --> 00:20:20.770
So let's create that.

00:20:20.770 --> 00:20:23.580
And then we also need
to create a destination

00:20:23.580 --> 00:20:25.082
rule and a virtual service.

00:20:25.082 --> 00:20:27.540
So let's look at our destination
rule, which actually we're

00:20:27.540 --> 00:20:28.440
already running.

00:20:28.440 --> 00:20:30.217
So this destination
rule just says

00:20:30.217 --> 00:20:32.550
that there's two subsets to
the product catalog service.

00:20:32.550 --> 00:20:33.750
There's a V1 and a V2.

00:20:33.750 --> 00:20:36.450
And those are selected based
on V1 and V2 labels, so very

00:20:36.450 --> 00:20:38.320
simple.

00:20:38.320 --> 00:20:40.750
And let's do a traffic split.

00:20:40.750 --> 00:20:45.690
So let's split traffic
50-50 between these.

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:48.173
Now, normally, you'd want
to do, like, a 1% or 0.1%

00:20:48.173 --> 00:20:49.590
or something very,
very small when

00:20:49.590 --> 00:20:51.690
you're canarying your
version, but here, we just

00:20:51.690 --> 00:20:52.815
want to show that it works.

00:20:52.815 --> 00:20:54.467
We're just going to
go to straight 50%.

00:21:00.680 --> 00:21:04.333
And now if we go back to our
typewriter, if we refresh,

00:21:04.333 --> 00:21:05.875
we should be able
to see-- yep, there

00:21:05.875 --> 00:21:07.400
it is-- a different version.

00:21:07.400 --> 00:21:08.895
So the product
description changed.

00:21:08.895 --> 00:21:09.645
The price changed.

00:21:09.645 --> 00:21:10.810
It went up a lot.

00:21:10.810 --> 00:21:12.892
New version, more
expensive typewriters.

00:21:12.892 --> 00:21:14.350
And if you keep
refreshing, you can

00:21:14.350 --> 00:21:17.750
see sometimes it'll going
to V1, sometimes go to V2.

00:21:17.750 --> 00:21:21.172
It just basically is flipping a
coin and deciding between them.

00:21:21.172 --> 00:21:22.630
So let's say we're
happy with that,

00:21:22.630 --> 00:21:27.970
and we decide, yep, let's
actually go full-on V2.

00:21:27.970 --> 00:21:28.750
So we can do that.

00:21:28.750 --> 00:21:31.010
We have a virtual
service that says,

00:21:31.010 --> 00:21:32.350
yes, send it all to subset V2.

00:21:32.350 --> 00:21:33.766
And let's apply that.

00:21:40.390 --> 00:21:42.940
And now if we go
to our boutique,

00:21:42.940 --> 00:21:46.410
our typewriter should
always be on V2.

00:21:46.410 --> 00:21:48.620
So that's a really
basic look at some

00:21:48.620 --> 00:21:51.030
of the network capabilities.

00:21:51.030 --> 00:21:54.560
Now let's look at our
final area here, security.

00:21:54.560 --> 00:21:56.450
And so we already have
mTLS set up, right?

00:21:56.450 --> 00:21:59.060
So these services
are all talking

00:21:59.060 --> 00:22:01.295
to each other over mTLS.

00:22:01.295 --> 00:22:02.420
They each have an identity.

00:22:02.420 --> 00:22:03.378
They have certificates.

00:22:03.378 --> 00:22:04.370
They're all set up.

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:07.280
But we're not actually
preventing any access.

00:22:07.280 --> 00:22:10.517
As long as something has a
certificate, it gets through.

00:22:10.517 --> 00:22:12.350
And so what we want to
do is be able to say,

00:22:12.350 --> 00:22:16.920
only the services we expect
to be calling are allowed to.

00:22:16.920 --> 00:22:20.870
So what we use is ASM's
authorization policy

00:22:20.870 --> 00:22:21.595
to do this.

00:22:21.595 --> 00:22:22.970
And first, what
we're going to do

00:22:22.970 --> 00:22:24.470
is show an authorization
policy that

00:22:24.470 --> 00:22:25.825
just locks it down completely.

00:22:25.825 --> 00:22:27.950
So that's a good first
step, although, clearly, you

00:22:27.950 --> 00:22:30.170
don't want to do this
production directly.

00:22:30.170 --> 00:22:34.550
But we can deny all traffic to
this product catalog service.

00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:37.710
And so all we have to do is
create an authorization policy.

00:22:37.710 --> 00:22:40.940
It has a selector that matches
that product catalog service,

00:22:40.940 --> 00:22:41.940
but it has new rules.

00:22:41.940 --> 00:22:43.520
So it doesn't allow any traffic.

00:22:43.520 --> 00:22:44.520
It just denies it all.

00:22:44.520 --> 00:22:45.770
So let's go ahead and do that.

00:22:50.600 --> 00:22:54.500
Once we deny everything, if
we go look at our boutique,

00:22:54.500 --> 00:22:59.050
it gives an access denied error,
which is what we'd expect.

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:01.690
So what about actually allowing
the traffic that we do want?

00:23:01.690 --> 00:23:04.300
Well, so here, we
have a different rule.

00:23:04.300 --> 00:23:07.280
This is, again, very simple.

00:23:07.280 --> 00:23:10.750
We have a rule that just
says, if the principle that

00:23:10.750 --> 00:23:14.020
is calling is the default
service account in the shop

00:23:14.020 --> 00:23:17.038
namespace, let it through,
and block everything else.

00:23:17.038 --> 00:23:18.580
So let's go ahead
and apply that one.

00:23:25.645 --> 00:23:29.640
And now if we go look at
our shop, it works again.

00:23:32.430 --> 00:23:34.310
And so that is
basically what security

00:23:34.310 --> 00:23:38.960
looks like for Anthos
Service Mesh with VMs.

00:23:38.960 --> 00:23:42.380
And so between that and
the traffic management

00:23:42.380 --> 00:23:43.940
that you get from
Anthos Service Mesh

00:23:43.940 --> 00:23:45.482
and the great
observability features,

00:23:45.482 --> 00:23:47.300
you can see just
how useful this can

00:23:47.300 --> 00:23:50.510
be for using virtual machines
with Anthos Service Mesh.

00:23:50.510 --> 00:23:51.010
Thanks.

00:23:51.010 --> 00:23:52.093
CHRIS CRALL: Thanks, Sven.

00:23:52.093 --> 00:23:53.450
That was a great demo.

00:23:53.450 --> 00:23:57.200
But I have a couple of questions
I've received from customers.

00:23:57.200 --> 00:23:59.780
The first one is, what
about a single VM?

00:23:59.780 --> 00:24:01.580
How do I handle that?

00:24:01.580 --> 00:24:03.770
SVEN MAWSON: Well, so the
great thing about MIGs,

00:24:03.770 --> 00:24:05.240
aside from all the great
features they offer,

00:24:05.240 --> 00:24:06.710
is that, actually, they're free.

00:24:06.710 --> 00:24:09.170
So you can just take
your VM, and you

00:24:09.170 --> 00:24:11.660
can extract an
image from it, which

00:24:11.660 --> 00:24:13.520
GCP makes very easy to do.

00:24:13.520 --> 00:24:18.710
And you can run that as a single
VM in a single managed instance

00:24:18.710 --> 00:24:19.292
group.

00:24:19.292 --> 00:24:20.250
And again, that's free.

00:24:20.250 --> 00:24:21.778
There's no extra
charge for that.

00:24:21.778 --> 00:24:22.820
And it's very easy to do.

00:24:22.820 --> 00:24:25.460
And then you can get all
the great features of MIGs,

00:24:25.460 --> 00:24:29.430
including the ability
to run and manage them.

00:24:29.430 --> 00:24:32.050
CHRIS CRALL: OK.

00:24:32.050 --> 00:24:34.890
And this is all great on GCE.

00:24:34.890 --> 00:24:37.260
But I have Anthos
on-prem, and I want

00:24:37.260 --> 00:24:39.010
to integrate my on-prem VMs.

00:24:39.010 --> 00:24:40.715
How do I do that?

00:24:40.715 --> 00:24:42.340
SVEN MAWSON: So we
are actively working

00:24:42.340 --> 00:24:46.870
on making that both supported
and very, very easy.

00:24:46.870 --> 00:24:49.780
It won't be quite as easy as
we've managed to make it on GCE

00:24:49.780 --> 00:24:52.360
because we don't have the
equivalent of managed instance

00:24:52.360 --> 00:24:53.350
groups.

00:24:53.350 --> 00:24:56.400
But we do have plans
in place to release

00:24:56.400 --> 00:24:58.480
a version of Anthos Service
Mesh with VM support

00:24:58.480 --> 00:25:01.600
for on prem, where you take
a couple more steps than what

00:25:01.600 --> 00:25:03.130
we've outlined here.

00:25:03.130 --> 00:25:05.380
You would be responsible
for generating

00:25:05.380 --> 00:25:07.000
the configuration
that goes on the VM

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.760
and installing the
HMM proxy there.

00:25:09.760 --> 00:25:11.917
And we provide
packages to do that.

00:25:11.917 --> 00:25:13.750
But then once you have
that, everything else

00:25:13.750 --> 00:25:15.250
will be the same
as described here.

00:25:15.250 --> 00:25:16.993
You can set up
the same features.

00:25:16.993 --> 00:25:18.910
It connects to the control
plane the same way.

00:25:18.910 --> 00:25:24.190
And you get all the great Anthos
Service Mesh capabilities.

00:25:24.190 --> 00:25:25.300
CHRIS CRALL: OK.

00:25:25.300 --> 00:25:27.390
And one last question--

00:25:27.390 --> 00:25:29.400
what if I have just VMs?

00:25:29.400 --> 00:25:33.270
My department or division isn't
using Kubernetes right now.

00:25:33.270 --> 00:25:34.350
I just have VMs.

00:25:34.350 --> 00:25:37.380
How do I set up the
mesh with those?

00:25:37.380 --> 00:25:39.900
SVEN MAWSON: So that is
a excellent question.

00:25:39.900 --> 00:25:44.820
So during the
architecture overview

00:25:44.820 --> 00:25:46.560
that I was giving
earlier, I was talking

00:25:46.560 --> 00:25:48.268
a little bit about
the different services

00:25:48.268 --> 00:25:49.410
that we need to connect to.

00:25:49.410 --> 00:25:51.810
And so three of those
services were still

00:25:51.810 --> 00:25:53.200
running in the cluster.

00:25:53.200 --> 00:25:56.160
So there was the
Istiod control plane.

00:25:56.160 --> 00:25:59.490
There was the
KubeDNS DNS resolver.

00:25:59.490 --> 00:26:02.103
And there was the API server
used for configuration storage.

00:26:02.103 --> 00:26:04.020
And the great news is
we're working on managed

00:26:04.020 --> 00:26:05.650
versions of all of those.

00:26:05.650 --> 00:26:07.578
So the control plane,
the managed version,

00:26:07.578 --> 00:26:09.120
is Traffic Director,
which is already

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:11.600
available through GCP's APIs.

00:26:11.600 --> 00:26:14.250
And we're working on making
it available through Anthos

00:26:14.250 --> 00:26:15.930
Service Mesh.

00:26:15.930 --> 00:26:19.320
For DNS, we're actively
working with the Cloud DNS team

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:22.890
to actually integrate the
Kubernetes DNS serving

00:26:22.890 --> 00:26:25.645
and GCE's DNS serving
into a single system.

00:26:25.645 --> 00:26:27.270
So actually, that
will all be built in.

00:26:27.270 --> 00:26:28.770
You won't need to
do anything extra.

00:26:28.770 --> 00:26:31.390
And again, it'll be a
completely managed service.

00:26:31.390 --> 00:26:33.900
And then, finally, we're
working on making it a managed

00:26:33.900 --> 00:26:35.650
version of the API server.

00:26:35.650 --> 00:26:37.500
So again, you don't
need to run anything.

00:26:37.500 --> 00:26:39.180
You can just use the
managed API server

00:26:39.180 --> 00:26:42.510
and use VMs with all of those
components fully managed

00:26:42.510 --> 00:26:44.538
by Google.

00:26:44.538 --> 00:26:45.330
CHRIS CRALL: Great.

00:26:45.330 --> 00:26:48.260
OK, well, thank you very much.

00:26:48.260 --> 00:26:50.060
SVEN MAWSON: Yeah,
great questions.

00:26:50.060 --> 00:26:51.860
And this was a lot of fun.

00:26:51.860 --> 00:26:53.890
Thanks very much.

